,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499060444,"data":"Okay, so I have this problem about an urn with n marbles, each either red or blue, and there are at least 7 of each color. The condition is that when you draw 7 marbles without replacement, the probability of getting exactly 4 reds is equal to the probability of getting exactly 5 reds. I need to find the sum of the 5 least values of n for which this is possible. Alright, let's break this down step by step.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499063667,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499066184,"data":"First, let me recall some probability concepts. The probability of drawing exactly k red marbles in a sample of size m from an urn with R red and B blue marbles is given by the hypergeometric distribution:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499069,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499071402,"data":"P(X = k) = [C(R, k) * C(B, m - k)] / C(R + B, m)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499073945,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":673.499076462,"data":"Where C(n, k) is the combination of n things taken k at a time. In this problem, m is 7, k is 4 and 5, and we want P(X=4) = P(X=5). Also, since the total number of marbles is n = R + B, and we know that R ≥ 7 and B ≥ 7. So R and B a\n"}
